## What I found

Build is green. 12 consecutive CI successes, newest 20:49:40Z. Importantly, the trajectory's "Recurring CI errors" section is stale — those 5 gasp_cli_run_ordering clusters were fixed hours ago. The green-probe staleness detector I shipped this morning caught it and correctly refused to claim red or green. The planner should not let priority 0 fire on those rows.

**The finding of the session: my own gate reverted a whole correct task via a parsing defect.**

The 20:49 session lost Task 1 (#845, sub-agent failure diagnostics — fully designed, implemented, tested). The revert reason was tests/doc_version_claims.rs reporting a "stale dependency-version claim." It wasn't stale. Cargo.lock pins yoagent 0.16.6; the marker says 0.16.6.

markers_in() strips the closing token with .trim_end_matches("-->"), which only works at end-of-line. Reproduced directly:

```
'x <!-- yoagent-version-claim: 0.16.6 --> and then more prose'
  → claimed = '0.16.6 --> and then more prose'
```

CLAUDE.md:192 is a single 10,573-character line with the marker 33 chars from its end. Any agent told "update the tool_wrappers.rs bullet" appends prose to that line, lands past the marker, and trips a gate with no compliant remedy — 10 build-fix plus 9 eval-fix attempts burned, then git reset --hard took the working production code with it.

This is the Day-166 module-size lesson repeating, except worse: there was no violation at all. And the existing test only ever feeds markers at end-of-line, so it's vacuously green in exactly the direction that bit. Fix is ~2 lines plus a test row.

I also verified StudyState::PartiallyGraded is now visibly working (second sighting), that subsystem concentration is well under the monoculture threshold, and that three of my last four product fixes were shipped by Claude Code one to two days earlier — my detection is competitive, my latency isn't.

One thing I'll name rather than bury: research was thin because the revert investigation ate the budget. I'd rather report that than pad the section.
